1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,720
Okay, moving on to nephritic syndrome.

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:04,920
The hallmark of nephritic syndrome is glomerular inflammation

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:07,080
and bleeding from the glomerulus.

4
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:09,420
Now, these patients can have limited proteinuria.

5
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:12,600
It's usually less than 3.5 grams per day.

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,100
That helps to distinguish this from nephrotic syndrome.

7
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:19,180
The patients can get oliguria, which is a decrease in urine production,

8
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:23,660
along with azotemia, which is an increase in nitrogenous waste products within the blood.

9
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,280
There is some salt retention.

10
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:28,340
The retention of salt will also result in the retention of fluid,

11
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:32,540
which can then result in edema, particularly around the eyes.

12
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:36,980
And that's because the minimal edema that develops particularly shows itself

13
00:00:36,980 --> 00:00:39,260
in the loose connective tissue around the eyes.

14
00:00:39,660 --> 00:00:42,900
In addition, because there's salt retention and fluid retention,

15
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:44,420
the patients develop hypertension.

16
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,920
Now, of course, I mentioned that a hallmark was glomerular bleeding,

17
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,780
and one of the key signs of glomerular bleeding will be the presence of red blood cell casts.

18
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:57,160
The idea here is that if this is the glomerulus, and the glomerulus is bleeding,

19
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:59,980
so you've got red blood cells coming into the tubules,

20
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,960
the red blood cells will get casted in the shape of the tubule,

21
00:01:04,240 --> 00:01:09,080
and then will be urinated out as this sort of square cast made up of red blood cells.

22
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:12,420
And again, that's because it gets casted in the shape of the tubule.

23
00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:16,140
So red blood cell casts is a sign of glomerular bleeding.

24
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,380
Now, if a biopsy were to be performed,

25
00:01:18,700 --> 00:01:21,340
we would see a hypercellular inflamed glomerulus.

26
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:24,700
And remember that I told you that the hallmark was glomerular inflammation.

27
00:01:25,360 --> 00:01:30,240
Important to note that this inflammation is usually driven by the deposition of immune complexes.

28
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,560
The immune complexes activate complement,

29
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:35,460
and one of the byproducts of complement is C5A.

30
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:38,200
Remember that C5A attracts neutrophils,

31
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:40,240
and the neutrophils then mediate the damage,

32
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:43,080
and that's why you get the hypercellularity in the glomerulus.

33
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:45,820
Here's a glomerulus from a patient with nephratic syndrome,

34
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:48,920
and we can see that there are way too many cells within the glomerulus,

35
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:54,320
and in addition, it's swollen, and it's filling most of Bowman's space.

36
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:57,600
The prototypic disorder that results in nephratic syndrome

37
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:59,580
is called post-strep glomerular nephritis.

38
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:05,340
Now, this is a nephratic syndrome that arises after a group A beta-hemolytic strep infection

39
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,360
of either the skin or the pharynx.

40
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:11,680
In particular, it occurs with nephratogenic strains.

41
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,000
So there are particular strains of group A beta-hemolytic strep

42
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,060
that carry a M protein virulence factor

43
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:21,840
that increases the risk of the development of nephritic syndrome.

44
00:02:22,320 --> 00:02:23,840
And so, again, it's important to note

45
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:26,440
that the M protein virulence factor

46
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:29,680
is what defines the nephratogenic strains.

47
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:32,840
It's also important to note that you can get a nephritic syndrome

48
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:35,820
after infection with non-strep organisms as well.

49
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,660
However, prototypically, it occurs after a group A beta-hemolytic strep infection.

50
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:43,260
The classic presentation of PSGN

51
00:02:43,260 --> 00:02:45,420
occurs two to three weeks after infection.

52
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:49,860
The patients present with hematuria, giving them a cola-colored urine.

53
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:54,520
And they can also develop oliguria, hypertension, and periorbital edema,

54
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:55,840
as I've already explained.

55
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:59,620
Again, it's usually seen in children, but it can also occur in adults.

56
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,200
On biopsy, you would see a hypercellular inflamed glomerulus.

57
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:06,360
Again, this is mediated by immune complex deposition,

58
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:08,640
so there would be a granular immunofluorescence.

59
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:11,280
And one of the hallmarks of this disorder

60
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:15,120
is that you have a sub-epithelial hump on electron microscopy.

61
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:16,620
Here's a picture of the EM.

62
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:18,840
This is a large sub-epithelial hump.

63
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:21,480
Notice that it's sitting on top of the basement membrane,

64
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:24,020
and this is where the epithelial cells would normally sit.

65
00:03:24,020 --> 00:03:26,080
Now, actually, what happens in this disorder

66
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:30,220
is that the immune complexes actually start sub-endothelial,

67
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,500
and then they slowly work their way up,

68
00:03:32,580 --> 00:03:35,060
and as they work their way up through this tri-layer,

69
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:38,120
they then pile up sub-epithelial.

70
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:39,880
And eventually what's going to happen is

71
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:43,480
this deposit will simply just dissipate into this space and disappear.

72
00:03:44,180 --> 00:03:45,620
And that helps me to remember

73
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:47,120
that the treatment is actually supportive.

74
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,300
You don't need to do anything.

75
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:51,300
The deposits will simply just pass their way through and then disappear.

76
00:03:51,300 --> 00:03:54,240
Now, in most children, the disease will actually resolve,

77
00:03:54,580 --> 00:03:56,400
and they rarely progress to renal failure.

78
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:59,900
However, adults do have a chance of progressing to renal failure,

79
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:01,580
and one of the feared complications

80
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:04,620
is something called rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.

81
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:07,220
Now, rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis

82
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:08,440
is a nephritic syndrome

83
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:11,620
that progresses to renal failure in weeks to months.

84
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:15,200
When a patient comes in with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis

85
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:17,540
and they've got signs and symptoms of a nephritic syndrome

86
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:21,020
and appear to be developing rapid renal failure,

87
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:22,640
a biopsy will be performed.

88
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:26,640
Now, the biopsy is characterized by crescents in Bowman's space.

89
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:28,460
And it's very important to note,

90
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:29,960
for the purposes of board exams,

91
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:33,520
that the crescent is made up of fibrin and macrophages.

92
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:36,580
Here's a picture of the glomerulus with the crescent.

93
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,440
Now, here I'm outlining for you the crescent,

94
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:41,540
and so this represents the crescent.

95
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:43,320
Here is the normal glomerulus.

96
00:04:43,960 --> 00:04:45,280
Now, examiners like you to know

97
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,660
that this stuff here in the crescent

98
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:50,000
actually is fibrin and macrophages,

99
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:51,940
so it's inflammatory debris.

100
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:53,600
And that's particularly high yield.

101
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,380
It's not collagen, etc.

102
00:04:55,600 --> 00:04:57,780
It is macrophages and fibrin.

103
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:00,620
Now, when a patient has rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis,

104
00:05:01,420 --> 00:05:03,480
the next thing that you're going to do after biopsy

105
00:05:03,480 --> 00:05:04,660
is immunofluorescence.

106
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:06,840
And the immunofluorescence will help you,

107
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:08,120
along with the clinical picture,

108
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:09,580
to resolve the etiology.

109
00:05:09,580 --> 00:05:11,300
So there's three possibilities

110
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:12,860
concerning the immunofluorescence.

111
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:15,080
The first possibility is that you get something

112
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:16,800
called the linear immunofluorescence.

113
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:18,520
Now, the idea here is that you've got

114
00:05:18,520 --> 00:05:19,900
these endothelial cells,

115
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:21,280
you've got the basement membrane,

116
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:23,260
and then, of course, you've got your podocytes.

117
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:26,100
If there is a linear immunofluorescence,

118
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:28,520
then that means that you have an antibody

119
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:30,640
against the glomerular basement membrane.

120
00:05:31,140 --> 00:05:32,620
This antibody then binds

121
00:05:32,620 --> 00:05:33,960
the glomerular basement membrane,

122
00:05:34,420 --> 00:05:37,020
creating a sharp line on immunofluorescence,

123
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:39,120
which is called a linear immunofluorescence.

124
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,340
Now, the key disease in which you see that

125
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:42,440
is Good-Pasture syndrome.

126
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:44,240
Of course, in Good-Pasture syndrome,

127
00:05:44,420 --> 00:05:46,240
you have an antibody against the collagen

128
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:47,680
in both the glomerular

129
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:49,620
and the alveolar basement membranes.

130
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:51,960
And so these patients will present with hematuria

131
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:53,180
due to the renal involvement,

132
00:05:53,620 --> 00:05:55,040
and they'll present with hemoptysis

133
00:05:55,040 --> 00:05:56,420
due to the pulmonary involvement.

134
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,640
It classically arises in a young adult male.

135
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,860
Now, another possibility would be

136
00:06:01,860 --> 00:06:03,860
to have a granular immunofluorescence.

137
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:05,360
And you're well aware of the fact

138
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:07,240
that when you have a granular immunofluorescence,

139
00:06:07,340 --> 00:06:08,920
you've got immune complex deposition.

140
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:10,480
The most common cause

141
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:12,360
if there's a granular immunofluorescence

142
00:06:12,360 --> 00:06:14,740
would be post-strep glomerulonephritis.

143
00:06:15,420 --> 00:06:17,160
And that's particularly important to note.

144
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:18,480
Again, in PSGN,

145
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:20,100
you've got antigen-antibody complexes

146
00:06:20,100 --> 00:06:21,080
which are depositing,

147
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:22,580
which then cause the disease.

148
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:25,440
Another very high-yield cause

149
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:27,800
if you had a granular immunofluorescence

150
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:28,920
would be something called

151
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,380
diffuse-proliferative glomerulonephritis.

152
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,500
Now, in diffuse-proliferative glomerulonephritis,

153
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,460
you get deposition of antigen-antibody complexes

154
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:39,600
that are sub-endothelial,

155
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:41,200
underneath the endothelial cell.

156
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:43,380
And what's very high-yield to note here

157
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:45,840
is that this is the most common type of renal disease

158
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:47,180
that you would see in lupus.

159
00:06:47,520 --> 00:06:48,700
Remember that in lupus patients,

160
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:50,700
the most common cause of death is renal failure,

161
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:53,120
and that the most common disease

162
00:06:53,120 --> 00:06:54,420
that damages the kidney

163
00:06:54,420 --> 00:06:57,280
is diffuse-proliferative glomerulonephritis,

164
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,440
giving you a nephritic syndrome,

165
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:00,620
and sometimes giving you

166
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:03,880
a rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.

167
00:07:04,540 --> 00:07:06,040
Now, it's also important to remember

168
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:07,680
that if a patient with lupus

169
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:09,200
presents with nephrotic syndrome,

170
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:11,540
remember that the cause of nephrotic syndrome

171
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:12,320
in lupus patients

172
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:14,180
is going to be membranous nephropathy.

173
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:16,000
Again, that's high-yield as well.

174
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:17,020
Now, finally,

175
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:19,180
if you have a negative immunofluorescence,

176
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:20,880
then they call that posse-immune.

177
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:23,160
Posse-immune means limited immune,

178
00:07:23,320 --> 00:07:25,860
i.e. you really don't have anything significant

179
00:07:25,860 --> 00:07:26,440
on immunofluorescence.

180
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:29,820
And so the next step in posse-immune cases

181
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:32,440
will be to perform something called an ANCA,

182
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:34,720
antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody.

183
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:36,520
Let's go back and remind ourselves

184
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:38,140
that when you perform the ANCA test,

185
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:39,960
you have a slide with a neutrophil on it.

186
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,940
Now, when you take the patient's serum

187
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:43,520
and you introduce it to this slide,

188
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:45,620
the antibodies in the patient's serum

189
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:47,600
can either bind adjacent to the nucleus,

190
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:50,460
or they can bind out here in the cytoplasm.

191
00:07:50,780 --> 00:07:52,480
If they bind adjacent to the nucleus,

192
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:53,240
then that is called

193
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,420
a perinuclear antineutrophil cytoplasmic.

194
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:57,960
Antibody, or a P-ANCA.

195
00:07:58,440 --> 00:08:00,300
If they bind out here in the cytoplasm,

196
00:08:00,380 --> 00:08:00,960
then that is called

197
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:05,340
a cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody.

198
00:08:05,880 --> 00:08:07,540
And, of course, in a posse-immune case,

199
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:09,500
the ANCA becomes particularly important.

200
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:11,280
The reason why is that patients

201
00:08:11,280 --> 00:08:12,360
who have a C-ANCA

202
00:08:12,360 --> 00:08:14,620
will have Wegner's granulomatosis.

203
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,380
Now, in Wegner's granulomatosis,

204
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,380
remember that you classically get involvement

205
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,420
of the lung, giving you hemoptysis,

206
00:08:20,820 --> 00:08:22,620
and you also get involvement of the kidney,

207
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,140
giving you rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.

208
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:28,980
But remember from our discussion of the vasculidities

209
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:32,000
that Wegner's granulomatosis is that C disease.

210
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:34,320
If you remember, I told you about the C mnemonic.

211
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:36,680
And so it's not only going to involve the lung

212
00:08:36,680 --> 00:08:37,520
and the kidney,

213
00:08:37,700 --> 00:08:40,360
but it's also going to involve the nasopharynx.

214
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:43,000
Now, this is very important for board examinations.

215
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:45,180
When a patient comes in with hemoptysis,

216
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:47,120
and they come in with hematuria,

217
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:50,260
and they've got a rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis

218
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:51,100
with crescents,

219
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:53,240
the knee-jerk is to think

220
00:08:53,240 --> 00:08:54,680
that they have Goodpasture syndrome,

221
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:56,100
because that's sort of the one

222
00:08:56,100 --> 00:08:57,220
that we classically remember.

223
00:08:57,640 --> 00:08:59,000
However, you need to be aware

224
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,880
that if the patient also has nasal symptoms,

225
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:04,360
nasopharyngeal symptoms like sinusitis,

226
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:06,280
they could tell you, for example, on the exam

227
00:09:06,280 --> 00:09:07,680
that the patient also has a history

228
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:09,720
of sinus infections or sinusitis.

229
00:09:10,160 --> 00:09:12,000
Then you really need to think about the fact

230
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,340
that you may be dealing with Wegner's granulomatosis,

231
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:16,480
because sinusitis or sinus problems

232
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:18,940
would not be seen in Goodpasture syndrome.

233
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:21,900
And, of course, the C-ANCA and the immunofluorescence

234
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:23,060
would help to distinguish this,

235
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:25,520
because you would have a linear immunofluorescence

236
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:26,260
in Goodpastures,

237
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:28,280
but you would have no immunofluorescence,

238
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:31,500
but instead a C-ANCA in Wegner's granulomatosis.

239
00:09:32,020 --> 00:09:33,800
Now, if the patient has a P-ANCA,

240
00:09:33,980 --> 00:09:35,120
then there's two possibilities.

241
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:38,420
One possibility would be microscopic polyangiitis,

242
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:40,900
and the other possibility would be Churg-Strauss.

243
00:09:41,340 --> 00:09:42,580
Now, what's important to note

244
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:44,480
is that in Churg-Strauss,

245
00:09:44,540 --> 00:09:45,660
you're going to have three things

246
00:09:45,660 --> 00:09:48,120
that you will not have in microscopic polyangiitis.

247
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:50,860
Number one, you'll have granulomatous inflammation.

248
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:53,620
That's not seen in microscopic polyangiitis,

249
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:54,900
but is seen in Churg-Strauss.

250
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:56,980
Number two, you'll have eosinophilia,

251
00:09:57,320 --> 00:09:59,580
which, again, would only be seen in Churg-Strauss.

252
00:09:59,780 --> 00:10:01,860
And number three, you'll have asthma.

253
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:04,700
And again, that would only be seen in Churg-Strauss.

254
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:07,460
And so, these are three important distinctions

255
00:10:07,460 --> 00:10:08,440
that you should note

256
00:10:08,440 --> 00:10:09,980
when you're trying to make the difference

257
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:13,400
between Churg-Strauss and microscopic polyangiitis,

258
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:16,060
again, both giving you a P-ANCA.

259
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:19,540
Here's a picture of the linear immunofluorescence.

260
00:10:19,660 --> 00:10:21,160
Notice that you've got immunofluorescence,

261
00:10:21,260 --> 00:10:23,360
but it contains these very sharp green lines.

262
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:24,940
And so, this would be an indication

263
00:10:24,940 --> 00:10:27,420
that you're most likely dealing with Goodpasture syndrome.

264
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:30,220
Okay, moving on to IgA nephropathy.

265
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:33,340
This is the most common cause of nephropathy worldwide.

266
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:35,120
In this particular case,

267
00:10:35,280 --> 00:10:36,940
IgA immune complex deposits

268
00:10:36,940 --> 00:10:38,960
in the mesangium of the glomeruli.

269
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:40,740
And so, this is what it looks like.

270
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:43,100
You get deposition in the mesangium,

271
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:46,260
but the most important is that this is IgA.

272
00:10:46,420 --> 00:10:47,600
And so, nothing else that we've covered

273
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:50,000
would give you IgA deposition in the kidney.

274
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:52,220
This is gonna present during childhood

275
00:10:52,220 --> 00:10:55,340
with episodic growth or microscopic hematuria.

276
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:57,160
You'll have RBC casts.

277
00:10:57,240 --> 00:10:58,860
Of course, if the glomerulus bleeds,

278
00:10:58,940 --> 00:11:00,720
then you're gonna have casting of the RBCs.

279
00:11:01,040 --> 00:11:03,000
And it usually follows mucosal infections.

280
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:04,600
Now, the idea here is

281
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:05,960
when you get a mucosal infection,

282
00:11:06,380 --> 00:11:08,620
you end up producing excess IgA

283
00:11:08,620 --> 00:11:09,880
to try to defend yourself

284
00:11:09,880 --> 00:11:11,260
against that mucosal infection.

285
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:13,440
So, every time you get a mucosal infection,

286
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:14,880
you produce a ton of IgA.

287
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:17,440
Some of that IgA goes into the mesangium.

288
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,440
It deposits as immune complexes

289
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:20,500
within the mesangium.

290
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:22,020
And then, that's gonna result

291
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:23,040
in the glomerular bleeding.

292
00:11:23,480 --> 00:11:24,680
Now, of course, it's episodic

293
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:27,000
because as the mucosal infection resolves,

294
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:28,600
you decrease the IgA.

295
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:30,880
And so, the hematuria goes away.

296
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:32,000
And then, the next time

297
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:33,080
you get a mucosal infection,

298
00:11:33,260 --> 00:11:34,640
you again get another episode

299
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:36,040
of bleeding from the kidney.

300
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:38,180
This can then slowly progress

301
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:39,220
to renal failure.

302
00:11:39,640 --> 00:11:40,600
I've already mentioned the fact

303
00:11:40,600 --> 00:11:41,560
that you're gonna have

304
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:43,220
IgA immune complex deposition

305
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:44,580
in the mesangium.

306
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:47,760
Alport syndrome is an inherited defect

307
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:48,860
in type 4 collagen.

308
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:50,500
It's most commonly X-linked.

309
00:11:50,840 --> 00:11:51,660
And what happens here

310
00:11:51,660 --> 00:11:53,040
is that you get thinning and splitting

311
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:54,480
of the glomerular basement membrane.

312
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:55,660
Now, when that occurs,

313
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:58,220
that's gonna result in isolated hematuria.

314
00:11:58,320 --> 00:11:59,680
You're only gonna have hematuria.

315
00:11:59,860 --> 00:12:01,680
But again, that's one of the hallmarks

316
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:02,540
of nephritic syndrome.

317
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:04,240
Now, the patients can also have

318
00:12:04,240 --> 00:12:05,280
sensory herring loss

319
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:06,500
because the basement membrane

320
00:12:06,500 --> 00:12:08,620
can be damaged in this region

321
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:10,540
along with ocular disturbances

322
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:11,660
also due to damage

323
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:12,880
of the basement membrane.

324
00:12:13,260 --> 00:12:15,100
And so, it's these three key findings

325
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:16,620
along with a family history

326
00:12:16,620 --> 00:12:18,760
that clue you in to Alport syndrome.

327
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,140
Isolated hematuria,

328
00:12:20,680 --> 00:12:21,620
sensory herring loss,

329
00:12:21,960 --> 00:12:23,020
and ocular disturbances

330
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:24,440
in a family.

331
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:26,340
And so, that concludes our section

332
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:27,340
on nephritic syndrome.

333
00:12:27,340 --> 00:12:28,480
Let's go.

334
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:32,600
We'll open up.

335
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:33,740
We'll open up.

